      housing: "Set the home up as though the cat has arthritis, because in this breed that assumption is safer than the alternative. Osteochondrodysplasia produces narrowed joint spaces and irregularly shaped bones of the lower limbs, with lameness and a stiff, inflexible tail as the visible indicators. That means ramps or steps to favorite perches instead of tall unassisted jumps, at least one litter box with a low-sided entry, food and water at floor level, and padded or orthopedic bedding. Warmth helps stiff joints, and the breed will happily curl up in a heated bed. Keep the cat indoors and keep flooring non-slip, since a cat with sore hocks and a sore tail quickly loses confidence on smooth surfaces. Vertical space still matters for enrichment, but it should be laddered rather than leapt.",
      diet: "Weight control is a joint-health intervention in this breed, not a cosmetic one. Feed measured meals rather than free choice, because obesity, roughly 20 percent or more above normal body weight, is the most frequently observed nutritional disorder in cats and contributes directly to musculoskeletal problems. Every extra pound is loaded onto joints that are already abnormal. Canned food supports both weight control and hydration, being higher in protein, lower in carbohydrate and about 90 percent water, which matters given the breed's polycystic kidney disease risk. If kidney or heart disease is diagnosed, diet becomes a prescription matter handled with your vet. Any weight loss plan should be gradual, roughly one to two percent of body weight per week.",
      enrichment: "Temperamentally this is an easy cat: affectionate and laid back, with a very even temperament, described as a comfortable, charming homebody that enjoys but does not crave human interaction. That makes it reasonably tolerant of being alone during a workday. The catch is that the same placid, undemanding nature masks pain. Sleeping more than usual, hesitation before jumping, poor grooming, reduced playfulness, changed litter box use and new aggression are all listed signs of osteochondrodysplasia, and a normally cuddly Fold that suddenly avoids being held may be hurting. So treat enrichment as monitoring: use ground-level wand play, puzzle feeders and low platforms, and log any decline in willingness to play, because that is your earliest clinical signal.",
      health: "Osteochondrodysplasia is the defining condition and it is not optional. Osteochondrodysplasia means abnormal development of bone and cartilage, and in this breed the folded ear and the skeletal disease are the same dominant TRPV4 mutation, so they cannot be separated by selective breeding. Early work suggested only cats with two copies were affected, but recent evidence indicates any cat with at least one copy can develop it, and the authors of the largest clinical study state that current findings suggest all Scottish Fold cats are affected to some extent. Severity and age of onset vary widely between individuals. On how often it becomes clinically obvious the evidence is more nuanced than the popular framing: a retrospective study of Australian primary-care records covering 1,131 Scottish Folds found a recorded clinical diagnosis in only 12 cats, or 1.1 percent, plus 69 suspected cases. Read that correctly, because it measures how often vets recorded a diagnosis rather than how many cats have the lesions, and diagnosis requires radiographs that most pet cats never get. The same paper reported a blinded radiologist assessment showing more extensive skeletal changes in Folds than in straight-eared Scottish Shorthairs. There is no treatment for the underlying condition, only pain management and home modification. Polycystic kidney disease is the second concern, an inherited progressive disorder in which fluid-filled cysts replace working kidney tissue: in a general Japanese hospital population of 1,281 cats, 6.90 percent of Scottish Folds carried the conventional PKD1 variant, and there is a DNA test. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is the third, affecting as many as one in seven cats overall, usually without outward signs, diagnosed by echocardiogram, and listed among the breed's genetic concerns.",
